"""Authored, task-oriented help. Protocol references are available in every panel."""

SOURCES = {
    "MPPT": "https://www.victronenergy.com/upload/documents/BlueSolar-HEX-protocol.pdf",
    "ORION": "https://www.victronenergy.com/upload/documents/Orion-XS-HEX-protocol.pdf",
    "BMV": "https://www.victronenergy.com/upload/documents/BMV-7xx-HEX-Protocol.pdf",
    "INV": "https://www.victronenergy.com/upload/documents/VE.Direct-HEX-Protocol-Phoenix-Inverter.pdf",
}

# Each entry explains the mechanism, a useful decision, and dependencies.
DETAILS = {
    "BAT_ABSORPTION_VOLTAGE": "The charger holds this voltage during absorption, after bulk charging has raised the battery voltage. A higher target increases charging pressure and can overcharge the battery; a lower target may leave it short of a full charge. Use the battery manufacturer's target for the complete battery bank. The duration and tail-current settings determine how long this stage lasts.",
    "BAT_FLOAT_VOLTAGE": "The voltage maintained after absorption to support a charged battery and connected loads. It is normally lower than absorption. A high float target can keep a battery charging when it should be resting. Use the battery manufacturer's float recommendation and check the absorption target before changing it. The re-bulk offset decides when falling voltage starts a new charge cycle.",
    "BAT_STORAGE_VOLTAGE": "The lower holding voltage used by chargers that support a storage stage. It reduces the time a fully charged battery spends at float voltage. Storage is a model-dependent feature; an unsupported response does not mean the charger is faulty. Use the battery's storage recommendation and keep this at or below float voltage.",
    "BAT_ABSORPTION_LIMIT": "The maximum time allowed for absorption, expressed in hours. This is a ceiling, not a promise that absorption will last this long. Adaptive timing, tail current, lack of input power, or external control can end or interrupt the stage. Choose the duration specified for your battery. Lengthening it can improve completion of charging but can also increase overcharge.",
    "BAT_BULK_LIMIT": "The maximum bulk-stage duration supported by the charger. Bulk uses available current to raise battery voltage toward absorption. A battery that cannot reach that target within the allowed time may have too much load, insufficient input power, or a fault. Changing this timer does not increase available charging power. Check the battery and power source before extending it.",
    "ADAPTIVE_MODE": "Lets the charger adjust absorption duration rather than use the same maximum duration every cycle. This can reduce unnecessary absorption after a shallow discharge. Its decision depends on the device's charge algorithm and battery conditions, so other chargers and loads can affect it. Turn it off when the battery manufacturer calls for a fixed absorption period; the maximum-time and tail-current settings still matter.",
    "BATTERYSAFE_MODE": "Enables the charger's BatterySafe stage where supported. The charger limits the rise in voltage while approaching absorption to reduce excessive gassing in suitable lead-acid batteries. This does not replace a correctly chosen battery preset or voltage target. Its availability and timing depend on the charger. Use the battery manufacturer's charging requirements.",
    "TAIL_CURRENT": "The charging-current threshold that can end absorption before its time limit. Lowering it usually requires the charging current to taper further; raising it can end absorption sooner. Zero disables current-based termination on supported chargers. Connected loads can keep charger output above the threshold even when the battery is full. This is a charger control, separate from a battery monitor's percentage-based full-charge detection.",
    "BAT_MAX_CURRENT": "The upper limit on battery charging current. The charger may supply less because of input power, temperature, battery voltage, or a BMS limit. Use the lowest permitted current for the battery, cabling, fuse, and charger. Several chargers can add their currents together, so account for all charging sources. Changing this setting does not increase the device's rated output.",
    "BAT_VOLTAGE": "The nominal battery-bank voltage used by the charging algorithm. Auto detection is available only where the device supports it. This is not the instantaneous measured battery voltage. A wrong nominal voltage can select inappropriate charging targets. Verify the actual series arrangement and the charger's supported voltage range, then check absorption and float values for that bank.",
    "BAT_VOLTAGE_SETTING": "Selects the nominal battery-bank voltage or automatic detection using the choices defined for this device family. This choice affects how the charger interprets its battery algorithm. Confirm the bank's series arrangement before selecting a voltage. Auto is useful only on models that support reliable detection for the connected bank. Read back the operating voltage after applying.",
    "BAT_TYPE": "Selects a factory battery algorithm or a user-defined algorithm. Preset numbers have different meanings on small MPPT, rotary-switch MPPT, RS, and Orion devices; VeDirWiz uses the selected family's choices. A preset can update other charging parameters inside the device. Read Charging again after applying a preset before editing individual voltage targets. Choose User for a custom curve approved by the battery manufacturer.",
    "BAT_TEMPERATURE_COMPENSATION": "Adjusts the charge-voltage target with battery temperature. The coefficient is in millivolts per degree for the entire bank, referenced to 25 °C. A negative coefficient raises the target when cold and lowers it when warm. The graph previews this relationship for absorption and float. Set zero when the battery's specification calls for no compensation. Remote battery-temperature sensing can provide a different temperature from the charger's internal sensor.",
    "BAT_LOW_TEMP_LEVEL": "The battery-temperature threshold used to restrict charging when the battery is cold. Some batteries, especially lithium types, cannot accept normal charging below a specified temperature. Set the threshold from the battery specification and make sure the temperature source actually measures the battery. The low-temperature current setting and BMS can also restrict charging; this setting cannot replace battery protection.",
    "LOW_TEMP_CHARGE_CURRENT": "Limits charging current under the device's low-temperature condition. The special Use maximum value removes this additional current restriction where supported. That is different from setting zero current. Choose a value permitted by the battery at low temperature and check the temperature threshold and sensing source. A BMS or the normal current limit can still impose a lower current.",
    "REBULK_VOLTAGE_OFFSET": "The amount below float voltage that contributes to restarting bulk charging. A larger offset waits for a deeper voltage drop; a smaller offset restarts charging sooner. The preview marks float minus this offset. The device may require the voltage to remain low for a time before restarting. Loads can create short voltage dips, so consider normal load behavior when choosing this offset.",
    "VOLTAGE_COMPENSATION": "The charger's voltage compensation allowance, where supported. Cable resistance can make voltage at the battery differ from voltage at the charger. This setting is model-dependent and is not a substitute for correct cable sizing or a real remote voltage measurement. Compare voltages at both ends under load and follow the device's documentation before changing it.",
    "BAT_EQUALISATION_VOLTAGE": "The target voltage for an equalization cycle. Equalization intentionally charges above normal absorption to service batteries designed for that procedure. Use it only when the battery manufacturer explicitly permits equalization. It can damage lithium and many sealed batteries. This target works together with the equalization current, duration, and stop condition; entering a target alone does not start a cycle.",
    "EQUALISATION_DURATION": "The maximum duration of an equalization cycle. The cycle can finish earlier if its voltage-based stop condition is enabled. A longer duration adds charging and heat, so use the battery manufacturer's procedure. Check the equalization target and current limit together. This setting has no effect while automatic equalization is disabled and no manual cycle is running.",
    "EQUALISATION_CURRENT_LEVEL": "The equalization-current limit as a percentage of the device's applicable maximum charging current. It limits the intensity of an equalization cycle; it is not a percentage of battery state of charge. Use the battery manufacturer's permitted equalization current and check the device's maximum current. The voltage target, duration, and battery suitability also determine whether a cycle is appropriate.",
    "AUTO_EQUALISE_STOP_ON_VOLTAGE": "Allows equalization to stop when its target voltage is reached instead of relying only on its maximum duration. This changes how long the battery experiences the equalization stage. Use the stop method specified for the battery. A reached target does not prove that every cell is balanced, and a timer limit still matters. This control does not enable or start equalization by itself.",
    "AUTOMATIC_EQUALISATION_MODE": "The automatic equalization interval in days on supported chargers. Zero disables automatic equalization. A nonzero interval schedules repeated equalization, so it must be appropriate for the battery rather than chosen as routine maintenance for every chemistry. Check voltage, current, duration, and stop mode before enabling it. Applying this interval does not represent a request to start a manual cycle immediately.",
    "BMS_PRESENT": "Tells the charger that a battery management system participates in charging control. This can change which conditions permit charging. Enable it only when the supported BMS and its control wiring or network are present and configured. The setting does not create a BMS connection or make an unprotected battery safe. Check the device state and off-reason readouts after applying.",
    "BMV_BATTERY_CAPACITY": "The capacity of the monitored battery bank in amp-hours. The monitor integrates current against this capacity to estimate remaining charge. Use the capacity rating relevant to the battery and discharge conditions. A wrong capacity makes state of charge and time remaining misleading even if measured current is correct. Parallel capacities add; series connections increase voltage without adding amp-hours.",
    "BMV_CHARGED_VOLTAGE": "The voltage the monitor must see before it can recognize a full battery. Full-charge recognition also requires current below tail current for the detection time. This threshold should match the real charging system's ability to bring the battery to full charge. Too high can prevent synchronization; too low can mark an incompletely charged battery as full. It does not change charger voltage.",
    "BMV_TAIL_CURRENT": "The current below which the monitor can recognize a full battery, expressed as a percentage of configured battery capacity. For example, 4% of 100 Ah is 4 A. Charged voltage must also be reached for the charged detection time. This changes the monitor's synchronization decision, not charger output. Set it to a threshold the charging system can actually reach at full charge.",
    "BMV_CHARGED_DETECTION_TIME": "How long charged voltage and tail current must both satisfy the monitor's full-charge conditions. Increasing the time filters brief qualifying periods; decreasing it synchronizes sooner. Choose a duration that matches the battery and charging system. If synchronization never occurs, inspect voltage and current thresholds before increasing the timer. A full-charge decision resets the monitor's charge estimate.",
    "BMV_CHARGE_EFFICIENCY": "The percentage of incoming amp-hours counted as stored charge. At 95%, the monitor counts 9.5 Ah from a measured 10 Ah charge. This compensates the charge estimate for battery losses. It does not improve the battery's physical efficiency. Use a value appropriate to battery chemistry and condition; the effect accumulates between reliable full-charge synchronizations.",
    "BMV_PEUKERT_COEFFICIENT": "Describes how usable battery capacity changes with discharge rate in the monitor's calculation. A value of 1 removes rate compensation. Larger values account for greater loss of usable capacity at high discharge current. Use a coefficient derived from the battery specification. This affects charge estimates and time remaining, not the measured current. The curve shows the modeled rate relationship relative to a 20-hour capacity rating.",
    "BMV_CURRENT_THRESHOLD": "Measured currents smaller than this magnitude count as zero in charge accounting. This prevents sensor noise near zero from accumulating into a false charge loss or gain. A large threshold also hides real small loads. Check the resting-current measurement and choose the smallest threshold that suppresses noise. Zero turns this filtering off. Calibrate the shunt only with all actual current removed.",
    "BMV_TTG_DELTA_T": "The averaging period for the current used in time-to-go estimates. A longer period steadies the estimate when loads vary; a shorter period responds faster to a new load. It does not alter measured current or physical battery capacity. Use a period suited to the normal load cycle, and expect time-to-go to change when the discharge floor or battery model changes.",
    "BMV_DISCHARGE_FLOOR": "The state-of-charge level used as the lower target for time-to-go calculations and applicable low-charge relay behavior. It represents the reserve you intend to keep, not a measurement of the battery's physical empty point. Choose a reserve permitted by the battery. A larger reserve shortens reported time remaining. This setting does not directly disconnect a load without a correctly configured control circuit.",
    "BMV_SHUNT_AMPS": "The rated current of the installed measurement shunt. Together with its rated voltage drop, it defines how the monitor converts the shunt signal into current. Use the value printed on the actual shunt. Incorrect scaling affects current, power, consumed amp-hours, and state of charge. This setting does not change the shunt's physical current rating or the installation's fuse requirement.",
    "BMV_SHUNT_VOLTS": "The rated voltage drop across the shunt at its rated current. VeDirWiz displays this in millivolts, matching shunt markings such as 50 mV. Match the installed shunt and its rated current. A wrong value scales every current measurement incorrectly. This is a calibration ratio, not battery voltage and not a threshold at which the monitor disconnects anything.",
    "BMV_TEMPERATURE_COEFFICIENT": "The capacity correction per degree used by a monitor with battery-temperature support. Battery capacity can change with temperature, especially for lead-acid batteries. This coefficient adjusts the estimate; it does not compensate charger voltage. Use the battery's capacity-versus-temperature data and verify the auxiliary input is set to temperature with a suitable sensor installed.",
    "BMV_AUXILIARY_INPUT": "Chooses what the auxiliary input measures: a starter battery, a bank midpoint, or temperature. These functions need different wiring and, for temperature, the correct sensor. Pick the function matching the physical installation. Alarm and display settings for the previous function may stop being meaningful after a change. Check the corresponding live measurement before relying on it.",
    "BMV_START_SYNCHRONIZED": "Controls whether the monitor assumes a synchronized charge state when it starts. Assuming a full battery at startup can be convenient in a system that always starts fully charged, but can overstate charge after a reset on a partly discharged bank. Choose the behavior that matches the installation. Automatic full-charge detection still needs correct charged-voltage, tail-current, and detection-time settings.",
    "BMV_DESCRIPTION": "A short name stored in the battery monitor, useful for identifying the bank. The device accepts up to 19 ASCII characters. Use a name that distinguishes this bank from other devices. Changing the name does not alter calibration or charging behavior. The name is included in exported settings and may be visible to other tools that read the monitor.",
    "DC_MONITOR_MODE": "Chooses battery monitoring or a supported DC energy-monitoring role. Battery mode estimates state of charge; other modes measure sources or loads and may not provide meaningful state-of-charge data. Pick the role that matches what is wired through the shunt. Review current direction and energy readouts after applying. This software selection does not change the physical wiring.",
    "LOAD_OUTPUT_CONTROL": "Chooses how the load output switches. Off and On request a fixed state; Auto uses the device's battery-life algorithm; user modes use configured voltage thresholds; AES uses its energy-saving timer. The lighting-timer switch adds the programmed night schedule. The device can still turn the output off for a fault or protection condition. Check load current, switch thresholds, and off reasons after applying.",
    "LOAD_SWITCH_LOW": "The lower battery-voltage boundary used by applicable user load-control modes. It lets the charger remove loads before the battery discharges too far. Set it below the reconnect boundary to create hysteresis, then choose a value suitable for the battery under load. The output's control mode must use these thresholds; Auto/BatteryLife has its own behavior.",
    "LOAD_SWITCH_HIGH": "The battery voltage at which applicable user load-control modes can reconnect the load. It should be above the disconnect boundary so voltage noise does not repeatedly switch the output. Battery recovery can raise voltage without a full recharge, so choose a useful recovery margin. The diagram shows the disconnect and reconnect boundaries. Current and fault protections remain active.",
    "LOAD_OFFSET_VOLTAGE": "The voltage offset used by supported load-control algorithms. It shifts a device-defined load boundary rather than setting the complete battery charging target. Its effect depends on load mode and firmware. Check the manufacturer's algorithm description and the high/low switch settings before changing it. Observe the load state and battery voltage through a full discharge and recovery cycle.",
    "LOAD_AES_TIMER": "The timer used by the load output's automatic energy-saving mode. It changes the device's AES timing, not battery capacity or charging duration. The setting matters only when an applicable load mode is selected. Use the device's AES documentation for the intended timing and verify that the connected equipment tolerates the resulting on/off behavior.",
    "PANEL_VOLTAGE_NIGHT": "The panel-voltage threshold used to recognize night for lighting control. Low panel voltage can also occur in shade, so use this together with sunset delay to avoid false switching. This is not the battery load-disconnect voltage. Choose thresholds that work with the installed panels and the device's daylight-detection behavior. The day threshold should be above the night threshold.",
    "PANEL_VOLTAGE_DAY": "The panel-voltage threshold used to recognize daylight for lighting control. A higher value waits for stronger panel voltage before treating the night as finished. Use it above the night threshold and combine it with sunrise delay to filter brief changes. This does not set the MPPT operating voltage or the panel's maximum safe voltage.",
    "SUNSET_DELAY": "The time night conditions must persist before sunset is recognized for the lighting schedule. A longer delay filters shade and short changes in panel voltage but starts the evening schedule later. It works with the night panel-voltage threshold. The actual event time can differ from astronomical sunset because the controller detects conditions at its solar input.",
    "SUNRISE_DELAY": "The time daylight conditions must persist before sunrise is recognized. A longer delay filters brief changes but can leave lighting on longer into the morning. It works with the day panel-voltage threshold and the events anchored to sunrise. The controller's detected sunrise can differ from an external clock or astronomical sunrise.",
    "MIDPOINT_SHIFT": "Moves the lighting controller's calculated midnight reference in minutes. Midnight is inferred from the night interval rather than assumed to be civil 00:00. Positive and negative shifts move midnight-anchored events in opposite directions. Use it to align the desired lighting schedule with the installation. Sunset- and sunrise-anchored events retain their own offsets.",
    "GRADUAL_DIM_SPEED": "Controls the transition timing when a supported lighting output changes brightness. Slower transitions reduce abrupt changes; faster transitions track schedule events more directly. The connected light driver must support the configured dimming output. This does not change a timer event's requested brightness. Check the TX output mode and the driver's electrical requirements.",
    "TIME_OF_DAY": "The device's time-of-day value in minutes after midnight, where supported. VeDirWiz presents it as a clock. The device may learn or update time through its lighting behavior or external control, so it is not a guaranteed persistent wall clock. Use the unavailable special value to represent no known time rather than entering a fictional measurement.",
    "INPUT_VOLTAGE_LOCKOUT": "Stops the Orion charger when input voltage drops below this boundary, helping protect the source battery. Input restart should be higher so the charger does not cycle on voltage noise. Cable voltage drop can trigger lockout under load. Choose boundaries for the source battery and measure voltage at the charger input while charging.",
    "INPUT_VOLTAGE_RESTART": "The input voltage required for Orion to recover from input lockout. Set it above the lockout threshold to provide a recovery margin. This is separate from engine-running detection: satisfying this boundary does not bypass the engine logic or BMS. Consider normal source voltage, alternator operation, and cable losses before applying.",
    "SHUTDOWN_VOLTAGE": "The source-voltage boundary used by Orion engine-shutdown detection. Below it, the charger treats the engine as stopped according to its detection algorithm. Set it for the alternator and source battery, not the battery being charged. Smart alternators can operate at low voltage, so the start, delayed-start, and delay settings must be considered together.",
    "START_VOLTAGE": "The Orion input voltage associated with engine-start recognition. This should correspond to a running alternator in the installation. A value too low can drain a starter battery with the engine off; a value too high can prevent charging with a smart alternator. It works with delayed-start voltage, start delay, and shutdown voltage.",
    "DELAYED_START_VOLTAGE": "The Orion input-voltage boundary that permits starting through the delayed engine-detection path. It supports alternators that do not always hold the immediate-start voltage. The start delay filters how long this condition persists. Use the actual alternator behavior to select it and keep enough margin above engine-off voltage to protect the starter battery.",
    "START_DELAY": "The wait used by Orion's delayed-start logic. Increasing it gives the source more time to stabilize after engine start; decreasing it starts charging sooner. It only matters when the input-voltage conditions for delayed start are satisfied. Review the delayed-start and shutdown thresholds together rather than using the delay to compensate for incorrect voltages.",
    "AC_OUT_VOLTAGE_SETPOINT": "The requested AC output voltage of the inverter. Match the connected equipment and stay within the device's published adjustable range. The actual output is a measured value shown separately. This device exposes the setting as write-only, so VeDirWiz can confirm command acceptance but cannot read the target back. Apply also saves Phoenix user settings to nonvolatile memory.",
    "INV_WAVE_SET50HZ_NOT60HZ": "Selects 50 Hz or 60 Hz AC output frequency. Match the connected equipment and installation. Changing frequency can affect motors, clocks, transformers, and other frequency-sensitive loads. The choice is write-only on this protocol; an accepted command is distinct from a measured frequency. Phoenix settings are saved when Apply completes.",
    "SHUTDOWN_LOW_VOLTAGE_SET": "The battery-voltage boundary that stops the inverter under the fixed cutoff algorithm. A loaded battery can sag below its resting voltage, so use the battery and load requirements when selecting it. The low-voltage warning and recovery levels should provide a sensible margin. Dynamic cutoff overrides this fixed shutdown boundary when enabled.",
    "ALARM_LOW_VOLTAGE_SET": "The inverter's low-battery warning threshold and a boundary relevant to restarting after low-voltage shutdown. Set it in relation to the shutdown and recovery thresholds, using the battery's permitted discharge behavior under load. Dynamic cutoff can override the fixed warning boundary. This target is write-only; the live alarm readout shows the resulting condition.",
    "ALARM_LOW_VOLTAGE_CLEAR": "The inverter's voltage threshold for clearing a low-battery condition and restarting after the applicable long-term shutdown. It should be above the warning boundary to avoid repeated restarts. A briefly recovered resting voltage may not mean the battery can support the load. Check the charger and battery before choosing an aggressive recovery threshold.",
    "AC_LOAD_SENSE_POWER_THRESHOLD": "The apparent-power boundary used by the inverter's load-detection behavior. Apparent power is expressed in VA and can differ from real watts. Choose a threshold that detects the smallest load you need without keeping the inverter active for unwanted standby demand. This works with the clear threshold and ECO search timing.",
    "AC_LOAD_SENSE_POWER_CLEAR": "The companion apparent-power boundary used when clearing a load-detection condition. Separating it from the detection boundary avoids repeated switching near one value. Small electronic loads can behave differently during ECO search pulses. Test the actual connected equipment with the threshold, retry time, and detection-period settings.",
    "INV_ECO_MODE_MIN_CURRENT": "The output-current setting used by supported inverter ECO load detection. It determines how sensitive search behavior is to small loads. Use the minimum useful load current for the installed equipment and verify startup in ECO mode. This is a detection setting, not the inverter's current rating or overload protection.",
    "INV_ECO_MODE_RETRY_TIME": "The interval used by the inverter when retrying load detection in ECO mode. Longer waits reduce search activity but delay powering a newly connected load. Shorter waits respond sooner. Some devices will not start from brief search pulses, so test the real load with the detection-period and threshold settings. The protocol uses quarter-second steps.",
    "INV_ECO_LOAD_DETECT_PERIODS": "The number of AC output periods used for each ECO load-detection attempt. This is a period count, not seconds. At 50 Hz one period lasts 20 milliseconds; at 60 Hz it lasts about 16.7 milliseconds. More periods give a load longer to respond but use more energy per attempt. Check the retry interval and actual load startup.",
    "INV_DYN_CUTOFF_ENABLE": "Makes the inverter's shutdown voltage depend on AC load current, allowing for battery voltage sag. It overrides the fixed shutdown and low-voltage warning targets. The live effective cutoff voltage is available as a measurement. The public HEX document names the coefficient settings without publishing their curve equation; VeDirWiz therefore does not invent a voltage curve from them.",
    "INV_NVM_COMMAND": "Runs a Phoenix settings-memory command when Apply is pressed. Save stores current settings; Revert loads the last saved settings; Backup loads the previous saved set when available; Default loads factory settings. VeDirWiz automatically sends Save after a normal Phoenix Apply. Revert, Backup, and Default can replace multiple settings, so read the device again after the command.",
    "DEVICE_MODE": "Selects the device's operating mode using the choices supported by the selected family. Turning a charger or inverter off can interrupt connected power even though communication remains available. ECO searches for loads; hibernate can slow or interrupt VE.Direct communication on supported inverters. This setting does not bypass external controls, a BMS, or protection faults. Inspect live state and off reasons after applying.",
    "DEVICE_FUNCTION": "Selects charging or power-supply operation where the device supports both. A power supply regulates an output rather than following the normal battery charging stages. Match the connected equipment, then review the applicable voltage and current controls. The setting does not convert an unsupported charger into a different product. Check measured output and device state after applying.",
    "BLE_MODE": "Enables or disables the device's Bluetooth radio where supported. Disabling it prevents Bluetooth tools from connecting, but a working VE.Direct cable can provide a way to turn it back on. This setting does not configure pairing, a PIN, or VE.Smart network membership. Read the device's Bluetooth capability flags before relying on recovery behavior.",
    "REMOTE_INPUT_MODE_CONFIG": "Chooses whether the remote terminals act as remote on/off control or a two-wire BMS interface on supported RS devices. Match the physical wiring and the battery's control signals. Selecting a BMS input without valid permission signals can stop charging or discharging. Check the two-wire BMS status and off reasons after applying.",
    "REMOTE_CONTROL_USED": "Declares which remote-control mechanism is in use through named switches. This is a device control configuration, not a physical measurement of a switch. Match the actual wiring and supported control method. Incorrect remote configuration can prevent operation or ignore an intended control. Check device state and off reasons after the setting is applied.",
    "RELAY_CONTROL": "Requests the relay's on or off state when its mode permits remote control. Reading the value reports relay state. An automatic relay mode can override or ignore a manual request, so choose Remote mode first if manual control is intended. The relay's normally open and normally closed contacts behave differently. Match the wiring and contact ratings of the connected circuit.",
    "RELAY_OPERATION_MODE": "Chooses the automatic function of the charger's relay. Available modes depend on the device's relay capability. Select the function for the wired circuit and then configure its corresponding set and clear thresholds. A relay can control an external contactor or alarm but cannot carry arbitrary load current. Read relay state and device faults to verify the intended behavior.",
    "BMV_RELAY_MODE": "Chooses the monitor's relay role. The default alarm role responds to configured conditions; charger and remote roles use their supported device behavior. Select the role matching the connected circuit, then check thresholds, inversion, and minimum on/off times. A changed mode can affect an attached generator or alarm. The relay-state readout shows what the monitor currently requests.",
    "INV_RELAY_MODE": "Chooses the Phoenix relay function, such as normal operation, warning/alarm status, low battery, fan, disabled, or remote control. The relay can switch an external control circuit, subject to its ratings. Choose the function that matches the wiring. In Remote mode the Relay control setting changes its state; other modes determine the state automatically.",
    "BMV_RELAY_INVERT": "Reverses the monitor's relay logic. This can be useful for a circuit that should signal a fault by releasing rather than energizing a contact. Confirm the normally open and normally closed wiring and the desired behavior on power loss. Inversion changes relay requests; it does not change the contact's electrical ratings or guarantee a fail-safe circuit.",
    "RELAY_MIN_ENABLED_TIME": "The minimum time the relay stays enabled once activated. A longer value avoids rapid cycling of connected equipment. It can also keep the circuit active after the original trigger clears. Review this delay with the relay function and threshold hysteresis, especially for generator or contactor control. It does not debounce incorrect sensor wiring.",
    "BMV_RELAY_DISABLE_TIME": "The minimum disabled period used by the monitor's relay control. It helps prevent rapid repeated activation after the relay turns off. Increasing it can delay a needed restart, so choose a value appropriate for the connected equipment. Consider it together with minimum enabled time and the set/clear thresholds.",
    "ALARM_BUZZER": "Enables the monitor's audible alarm where the hardware has a buzzer. Disabling sound does not remove alarm conditions or change thresholds. Visual status and relay behavior can still report an alarm. Choose whether local sound is useful for the installation and test the relevant alarm condition; remote monitoring should still use the alarm-state readout.",
    "TX_PORT_MODE": "Selects the alternate function of the device's TX pin, such as normal communication, energy pulses, PWM dimming, or a virtual load output. Alternate modes can affect the VE.Direct interface. Match the attached equipment and electrical interface; a mode choice alone does not supply a suitable cable or driver. Record a recovery method before changing communication-pin behavior.",
    "RX_PORT_MODE": "Chooses how the device interprets its RX input, including supported remote and load-control functions. An alternate input function can affect normal VE.Direct communication. Match the wiring and active signal polarity, particularly for inverted modes. Check the load state and remote-control behavior after applying. Incorrect input selection can leave equipment permanently on or off.",
    "NETWORK_MODE": "Configures named participation and control roles for VE.Smart or external charging coordination. These switches can move responsibility for voltage, current, or state to another controller. Use only a complete, supported control setup with valid measurements and a functioning controller. Enabling a role does not pair devices. Check network status and sensing flags before relying on coordinated charging.",
    "CHARGE_VOLTAGE_SETPOINT": "The voltage target supplied through external or synchronized charge control. This can override the local charging algorithm when the appropriate control mode is active. It is not a saved battery preset. A controller must keep its requests valid and within battery limits. Standalone chargers report Unavailable rather than a battery voltage; that is not 655.35 V. Use the normal Charging window for standalone charging; inspect network status if this target is in use.",
    "BATTERY_VOLTAGE_SENSE": "An externally supplied battery-voltage measurement used by supported charging control. This is an input measurement, not a desired charge voltage. A stale or invented value can lead to incorrect regulation. A real sensing controller should keep it updated. The Unavailable option stops representing it as a valid measurement where the protocol supports that sentinel.",
    "BATTERY_TEMPERATURE_SENSE": "An externally supplied battery temperature used by supported charge control. It should come from a valid sensor on the battery, with the external controller maintaining freshness. It can affect voltage compensation and cold-charging limits. Do not enter ambient temperature as a permanent replacement for a sensor. Unavailable represents no valid external temperature where supported.",
    "BATTERY_CHARGE_CURRENT": "The battery-current measurement supplied to supported coordinated charging. This can differ from a charger's own output because loads and other chargers also contribute. Its sign and accuracy matter to charge termination. Use an active measurement source that follows the device's protocol requirements. Standalone chargers report Unavailable rather than a huge current. A manually entered value is not a persistent current sensor.",
    "TOTAL_CHARGE_CURRENT": "The total charging-current value supplied through the device's external-control interface. It represents coordinated current information, not necessarily the measured output of this one charger. Only set it as part of a controller that keeps it updated. Standalone chargers report Unavailable rather than a huge current. Local charging limits and a BMS remain relevant. Check network roles and measured battery current to interpret the result.",
    "CHARGE_CURRENT_LIMIT": "The charging-current ceiling supplied by external or synchronized control. It can restrict current below the local maximum when that control path is active. This is not the device's own maximum charge current. Standalone chargers report Unavailable rather than thousands of amps. Use a supported controller and a limit permitted by the battery and wiring. The actual output may still be lower because of power or temperature.",
    "CHARGE_CURRENT_PERCENT": "A percentage request used by supported network charge control. It scales an applicable device current limit rather than specifying an absolute battery current in amps. Its exact use depends on network roles and the charger. Use it as part of a complete control implementation, then inspect actual current and external-control status.",
    "CHARGE_STATE_ELAPSED": "The elapsed state time supplied by external charge coordination, expressed in milliseconds. It describes time already spent in a charging state rather than setting the maximum absorption duration. Changing it can affect a controlled charger's stage timing. Use it only with a controller that also supplies consistent charge state and targets.",
    "ABSORPTION_TIME": "The absorption timing value supplied through external or synchronized charging. This belongs to the coordination interface and is separate from the local maximum absorption setting. A controller using it must maintain consistent charge state, elapsed time, and voltage targets. For a standalone charger, configure the Charging window and let the local algorithm manage the stage.",
    "NETWORK_ERROR_CODE": "An error status supplied through the coordination interface. This is not a command to repair or erase a physical charger fault. An external controller should report the actual condition defined by the device protocol. Use the live charger error and network status to diagnose faults. Writing a misleading status can cause other participants to make incorrect decisions.",
    "BATTERY_IDLE_VOLTAGE": "The battery resting-voltage value supplied to coordinated charging. Some charge algorithms use the initial battery condition to choose stage duration. This should be a valid measurement taken under the required idle conditions, not an arbitrary desired voltage. Standalone chargers report Unavailable rather than a battery voltage. Use it only within a controller that understands those conditions and maintains the rest of the charge state.",
    "LINK_DEVICE_STATE": "The state supplied through a synchronized charging link. It can direct coordinated stage behavior and should agree with supplied voltage, current, and timing values. This is separate from the read-only state reported by the physical device. Use it only as part of a supported controller; changing it alone does not create a working charge network.",
    "TOTAL_DC_INPUT_POWER": "The total DC input-power value supplied through supported external coordination. It is control information, not a locally measured panel-power reading. Use a controller that calculates and updates the actual value. Incorrect information can distort coordination. For monitoring, use the measured panel or input power shown on the live overview.",
    "SETTINGS_CHANGED_TIMESTAMP": "A Unix timestamp that external applications can use to record when settings changed. It does not change the device clock or charging behavior. Some firmware reports an unavailable value before a timestamp has been written. Use it only if the applications managing this installation agree on its meaning. VeDirWiz's own activity log independently records Apply results.",
    "RESTORE_DEFAULTS": "Restores the device's user settings to factory defaults when Apply is pressed. This replaces your configured targets and modes and can change power operation. Factory calibration is preserved by the documented command. Export current settings first if they are needed later. VeDirWiz stages this command separately, requires acknowledgement of its effect, and requires a fresh read afterwards.",
    "CLEAR_HISTORY": "Clears the device's resettable history when Apply is pressed. Lost historical records cannot be reconstructed by reading the device again. Export history first if you need it. This command does not fix a battery, change charging targets, or erase every lifetime statistic on every model. VeDirWiz requires acknowledgement of the data loss before sending it.",
    "BMV_SYNCHRONIZE": "Sets the monitor's charge estimate to fully charged when Apply is pressed. Use this only when the bank really is full. Synchronizing a partly charged bank makes later state-of-charge and time-to-go estimates optimistic. It does not charge the battery. Check capacity and automatic full-charge detection settings so future synchronizations can occur correctly.",
    "BMV_ZERO_CURRENT": "Calibrates the monitor's zero-current offset when Apply is pressed. Disconnect or turn off every charging source and load so no real current flows through the shunt. Calibrating with current present creates an offset that accumulates errors in consumed charge and state of charge. The current threshold is a separate noise filter and cannot repair a bad zero calibration.",
    "BMV_ALARM_ACKNOWLEDGE": "Acknowledges the battery monitor's alarm when Apply is pressed. Acknowledgement can silence or mark an alarm without removing the electrical condition that triggered it. Inspect the live alarm reason and the relevant measurement. Correct the cause or threshold as appropriate. This command does not reset history or synchronize the battery's charge state.",
    "REMOTE_COMMAND": "Stages a supported charger command, such as starting or stopping manual equalization or synchronizing a device function. It runs once when Apply is pressed. Starting equalization requires a battery that explicitly permits it and correctly configured voltage, current, and duration. Check the device state after the command; acceptance does not mean a full charging cycle has completed.",
}

ALIASES = {
    "MPPT_SMALL_BAT_TYPE": "BAT_TYPE", "MPPT_RS_BAT_TYPE": "BAT_TYPE", "ORION_BAT_TYPE": "BAT_TYPE",
    "INV_DEVICE_MODE": "DEVICE_MODE", "ORION_DEVICE_MODE": "DEVICE_MODE", "BMV_CLEAR_HISTORY": "CLEAR_HISTORY",
}


def explanation(reg, label, group):
    name = reg.name
    key = ALIASES.get(name, name)
    if key in DETAILS:
        body = DETAILS[key]
    elif name.startswith("TIMER_EVENT_"):
        body = "A brightness change anchored to detected sunset, calculated midnight, or detected sunrise. The offset is in minutes: a negative value places it before the anchor and a positive value after. Brightness is a percentage from off at 0% to full at 100%. Drag the point on the schedule or edit its timing and level. Six event slots are available. The schedule acts only when the load's lighting timer and a compatible dimming output are configured. The timeline assumes 18:00 sunset and 06:00 sunrise for preview; actual times follow the device's solar detection."
    elif name.startswith("INV_DYN_CUTOFF_FACTOR"):
        body = "A coefficient of the Phoenix dynamic cutoff algorithm. The firmware uses these factors with load current to determine an effective battery shutdown voltage. The public protocol does not define their conversion to voltage/current points, so these are coefficient controls, not guessed curve coordinates. Use model-specific calibration data. The live effective cutoff can be plotted while testing actual loads. These write-only values cannot be recovered from a normal device read or a fresh settings export."
    elif any(part in name for part in ("_ALARM_", "_RELAY_", "RELAY_BAT_", "RELAY_PANEL_")) and reg.cls == "NUMERIC":
        low = "LOW" in name
        clear = "CLEAR" in name
        subject = "state of charge" if "SOC" in name else "battery temperature" if "TEMPERATURE" in name else "starter-battery voltage" if "STARTER" in name else "bank midpoint deviation" if "MID_VOLTAGE" in name else "panel voltage" if "PANEL" in name else "battery voltage"
        direction = "below" if low else "above"
        action = "clears the condition after recovery" if clear else "activates the condition"
        body = f"This {subject} boundary {action}. The trigger acts when the measurement moves {direction} its threshold; the clear boundary provides a separate recovery point. Keep low-condition recovery above its trigger and high-condition recovery below its trigger to prevent rapid toggling. "
        body += "Alarm thresholds affect reported alarms and optional sound; relay thresholds affect the circuit selected by the relay mode. They do not directly change charging voltage. "
        body += "Zero disables applicable BMV voltage or charge thresholds; temperature thresholds have an explicit Disabled option. " if name.startswith("BMV_") else "Use the selected relay mode's applicable thresholds. "
        body += "Choose limits from the battery and connected equipment specifications, and verify them against live measurements."
    elif "DISPLAY" in name or name.startswith(("BMV_SHOW_", "BMV_BACKLIGHT", "BMV_SCROLL", "BMV_SETUP_LOCK", "BMV_TEMPERATURE_UNIT")):
        if "SHOW_" in name:
            body = f"Chooses whether {label.lower().replace('show ', '')} appears in the battery monitor's local display cycle. Turning it off hides that display item but does not stop measurement or logging over VE.Direct. Show the values useful to someone at the device. An item still requires the appropriate sensor and monitor mode to provide meaningful data. This is separate from VeDirWiz's own dashboard."
        elif "LOCK" in name:
            body = "Locks or unlocks setup from the device's physical display where supported. Locking helps avoid accidental changes at the panel. It is not a password or a security boundary for VE.Direct access. Keep a working cable connection if you need to unlock it later. The choice does not lock VeDirWiz's own editing controls."
        elif "TEMPERATURE_UNIT" in name:
            body = "Chooses Celsius or Fahrenheit for the device's own display. This is a display preference and does not change the battery-temperature sensor or charge compensation. VeDirWiz presents temperature settings in Celsius and performs the protocol conversion independently. Check that a temperature sensor is installed when you expect a temperature readout."
        elif "SCROLL" in name:
            body = "Controls how quickly the physical display cycles through its information. Choose a slower speed for reading values at a glance or a faster speed when checking several items. Documented endpoints are Slow and Fast; firmware also uses numbered intermediate speeds 2, 3, and 4. It does not change VE.Direct telemetry sampling or VeDirWiz's graph interval."
        elif "CONTRAST" in name:
            body = "Adjusts the device display's contrast where supported. Choose a level readable at the installation's temperature and viewing angle. This does not change backlight brightness or any charging behavior. VeDirWiz's appearance is independent of the physical display setting. Unsupported displays report that this control is unavailable."
        else:
            body = "Controls the physical display's backlight behavior or intensity where supported. Automatic or keypress behavior can reduce idle light and power use; an always-on setting keeps the display visible. Select a level suitable for the installation. Some displays offer only off and on rather than a brightness percentage. This does not change VeDirWiz's theme or device operation."
    elif reg.access in ("READ_ONLY", "CONSTANT"):
        body = f"{label} is reported by the device and cannot be edited here. "
        if group == "history":
            body += "History records describe earlier device operation rather than the current instant. Select Read values to retrieve the records; different models retain different periods and statistics. Unavailable fields stay empty instead of being counted as zero. Export the data before clearing history. Day zero is the current day; higher day indexes refer to earlier days."
        elif reg.cls == "BITMASK":
            body += "Several named conditions can be active at the same time. A clear list means no reported flags, while an unavailable value means no valid read has been made. Use the named conditions with the related measurements to diagnose why the device is limited or stopped. A software status cannot establish that physical wiring is correct."
        elif reg.cls == "NUMERIC":
            body += f"The value uses {reg.unit or 'the unit indicated by the device definition'}, with protocol scaling already applied. Read values requests a fresh value. Check its timestamp before interpreting it; an old cached value does not describe a disconnected device. Compare limits and ratings with the relevant measured values. Not every model in a family implements every readout."
        else:
            body += "Read values requests fresh device information. Identity, capability, and version values help establish which functions the hardware and firmware support. A catalog entry alone does not prove support. An unavailable or rejected read stays visible with its reason. Export includes the values actually read and their timestamps."
    else:
        raise ValueError(f"Missing authored help for {name}")
    access = "This command runs only when Apply is pressed." if reg.cls == "VOID" or name in ("INV_NVM_COMMAND", "REMOTE_COMMAND") else "Edits stay pending until you press Apply."
    if reg.access == "WRITE_ONLY":
        access += " The device exposes this as write-only; command acceptance can be checked, but the stored value cannot be read back."
    if reg.access in ("READ_ONLY", "CONSTANT"):
        access = "This is a readout. Read values updates the cached value."
    return {"what": body, "workflow": access, "source": SOURCES["BMV" if "BMV" in reg.flavors else "ORION" if "ORION" in reg.flavors else "INV" if "INV" in reg.flavors else "MPPT"]}


CONTROL_HELP = {
    "live": "The trace plots a selected measured value over the chosen maximum time window. The horizontal scale fits the received data until that window is full. Pause freezes drawing while collection continues. CSV export saves up to the latest 900 received frames; it is not an unlimited recording. Values from alternating device frames are retained with individual timestamps. Power uses the device's reported panel, DC, or AC reading where available, otherwise battery voltage times current. Meter scales help visualize a number and do not define safe operating limits.",
    "connection": "Choose the family matching the physical device before connecting. Small MPPT models use a different battery preset table from rotary-switch models. Serial communication normally uses 19200 baud. Demo opens a simulated device and never touches a serial port. Changing connections requires discarding pending edits so settings cannot transfer to the wrong device.",
    "apply": "Review the old and new values, then apply them to this connection. VeDirWiz checks readable settings for external changes before writing, sends one setting at a time, and reads back ordinary settings. The protocol has no atomic multi-setting transaction. A failure can leave earlier writes in place. The result identifies accepted, verified, failed, and unattempted changes. Phoenix settings also require a Save command, which normal Apply sends automatically.",
    "profiles": "Export stores readable settings and known accepted write-only targets in a JSON file with the device family. Unread write-only values cannot be recovered. Import checks the family and field types, then stages changes for review. It never applies automatically. Profiles omit one-time commands and transient external-control inputs. A matching family does not guarantee matching hardware ratings; review every imported voltage and current limit.",
    "temperature": "The graph previews voltage = target at 25 °C + coefficient × temperature difference. The coefficient is converted from millivolts to volts. Drag the absorption endpoint to adjust the coefficient. This preview is a linear relationship, not a prediction of sensor temperature or every firmware clamp. Read the temperature and target settings before using it.",
    "chargecurve": "Drag the absorption or float point to change its voltage target; move the absorption-end point horizontally to change maximum absorption time. The rising bulk segment is illustrative because bulk duration depends on input power and battery condition. The graph uses the device's voltage and duration values and updates pending settings. Adaptive timing and tail current can end absorption sooner than the displayed maximum.",
    "lighting": "The curve shows six brightness events relative to sunset, midpoint, or sunrise. Drag a point to change its offset and brightness. The preview uses sunset at 18:00 and sunrise at 06:00; the physical device detects its own night. The table gives the actual anchor and minute offset written to each event. Configure the lighting timer and the output driver as well as these events.",
    "peukert": "This educational curve uses the monitor's capacity and exponent with a 20-hour rating to show how discharge current changes modeled runtime. Drag the endpoint to adjust the exponent. Battery rating conditions, temperature, age, efficiency, and the chosen discharge floor affect real runtime. It is a model preview, not a guarantee or a measurement.",
    "dynamic": "The public Phoenix protocol exposes dynamic cutoff coefficients but does not publish the equation needed to map them to a voltage curve. VeDirWiz graphs the measured effective cutoff against measured load current instead. Read the cutoff and current repeatedly while varying actual loads to observe the firmware's behavior. Coefficients remain editable with full help and explicit write-only status.",
}
